    "passage": "India’s newest low-cost airline, Akasa Air, is making news even before its first flight takes off. The airline recently unveiled its complete first look of its airline crew uniform. [[1]] immediately grabbed people’s attention is how the design prioritizes comfort over fashion. Rather than the typical high heels one would expect to see flight attendants wear, the cabin crew members [[2]] to showcase black-and-orange sneakers. It is indeed a first for any Indian airline, and such comfortable and easy clothing style is certainly well-received by many netizens.\n[[3]] are the uniforms comfortable, but they are eco-friendly as well. According to Akasa Air, the trouser and jacket fabric has been made using recycled polyester fabric, which is made from PET bottle plastic salvages from marine waste. “The uniform fit [[4]] providing the best possible stretch to ensure employees’ comfort over their busy flight schedules.” Deepika Mehra, Founder of the shoe company Vanilla Moon, added that the shoes are “sustainably produced, functional, comfortable, gender-neutral and contemporary.” It also reflects the airline’s employee-centric culture, which [[5]]. Akasa Air received the delivery of its first Boeing 737 max aircraft in India on June 21. The airline is now gearing up for its commercial launch by the end of July.",
